      // [src_schop_sep, src_schop_iep, src_schop_eb, src_schop_eb1911 | 1819-1853 | scholarly encyclopedia x2 + admiring biographer + older encyclopedia] The major work made no impression on the public (IEP) and two Berlin attempts to lecture failed (Britannica), yet he kept writing and publishing: On the Will in Nature (1836), the prize essay (1839), a doubled second edition (1844) that again won no acclaim (IEP), Parerga (1851) and a revised third edition in 1859; recognition began only after 1853 (SEP). Four accounts document more than three decades of continued work without audience. RUBRIC_CORRECTION (lead review, before eligibility): persistence lowered from 78 / 0.64 to 74 / 0.6 because the same reference-source concentration; the lecture failure and lawsuit are retold briefly.
