      // [src_schop_sep, src_schop_iep, src_schop_eb, src_schop_eb1911 | 1818-1841 | scholarly encyclopedia x2 + biographer + older encyclopedia] Scheduled his Berlin lectures against Hegel's hour (SEP, IEP, Britannica), wrote so rudely to his publisher that Brockhaus broke off correspondence (Britannica 1911), refused a family creditor compromise and recovered more by holding out (Britannica 1911), lost a five-year lawsuit brought by a fellow lodger after a quarrel over noise (SEP, IEP) and, when the Danish Academy gave his essay no prize, published it with an introduction attacking the Academy (IEP, SEP). Several independent accounts show a repeated readiness to confront rather than avoid; the costs (lost suit, no audience, broken correspondence) make the impact dual-edged. Scored for the behaviour, not for any temperament label. RUBRIC_CORRECTION (lead review, before eligibility): conflict_tolerance lowered from 82 / 0.66 to 78 / 0.62 because the episodes are documented but the four sources are encyclopedias retelling the same few anecdotes, and the 1911 article colours them.
      conflict_tolerance: [78, 0.62, "d", "D"],
      // [src_schop_iep, src_schop_eb1911, src_schop_eb | 1815-1825 | scholarly encyclopedia + older encyclopedia + biographer] His joint interest with Goethe in colour ended when he published a theory he presented as superior and Goethe took him for an opponent (IEP, Britannica 1911); his first publisher Brockhaus broke off correspondence after his rude letters (Britannica 1911), and his two attempts to establish himself as a Berlin lecturer failed (Britannica); the sources show no sustained working partnership, circle of collaborators or school. Inferred from a pattern of ended working relationships; each episode is told from one or two sources, so confidence is moderate-low.
      collaboration: [36, 0.5, "s", "D"],
      // [src_schop_sep, src_schop_eb, src_schop_eb1911 | 1814-1860 | scholarly encyclopedia + admiring biographer + older encyclopedia] SEP describes a deliberate daily routine over twenty-seven years in Frankfurt (morning reading and study, flute, lunch at the same inn, afternoon walk, evening reading); Britannica says he gave three years exclusively to the main work and the 1911 article that he read and took notes with regularity in Dresden. Routine documented by one source for Frankfurt, supported for the earlier period by two. RUBRIC_CORRECTION (lead review, before eligibility): discipline lowered from 74 / 0.6 to 70 / 0.54 because the daily-routine evidence is from an admiring biographer and an old encyclopedia.
      discipline: [70, 0.54, "d", "A"],
      // [src_schop_eb, src_schop_sep, src_schop_eb1911 | 1815-1825 | admiring biographer + scholarly encyclopedia + older encyclopedia] Britannica says the three years in Dresden were devoted exclusively to preparing and writing the main work; SEP records that the quarrel with his neighbour arose because loud conversation made it difficult for him to concentrate on his work; the 1911 article notes that he read and took notes with regularity. A pattern of protecting long uninterrupted work, inferred from three accounts; no direct description of his working method.
      deep_focus: [68, 0.54, "s", "A"],
      // [src_schop_iep, src_schop_eb, src_schop_sep | 1819-1859 | scholarly encyclopedia x2 + admiring biographer] IEP says his system remained virtually unchanged from its first articulation and Britannica says that after the main work there were no inner struggles, changes or critical reorganisation of basic thoughts, only more detailed exposition; SEP describes On the Will in Nature (1836) as an effort to confirm his views by scientific evidence, and the 1844 second edition added a second volume to the original text. Held below the middle because the sources agree that later work extended and defended rather than revised; the judgement partly rests on an admiring biographer's summary, so confidence is moderate-low.
      belief_updating: [32, 0.5, "s", "D"],
      // [src_schop_sep, src_schop_eb, src_schop_iep | 1809-1851 | scholarly encyclopedia x2 + biographer] SEP lists university courses in physics, psychology, astronomy, zoology, archaeology, physiology, history and literature, and his later work spans metaphysics, ethics, aesthetics, a colour theory, scientific confirmations of his philosophy, a study of Sinology and Eastern texts and a late collection of popular essays (Parerga); Britannica and IEP note his sustained reading of Indian thought. The breadth is documented, but the writing returns to one central doctrine, which holds the score in the low 70s.
      cross_domain_range: [72, 0.58, "s", "A"],
      // [src_schop_iep, src_schop_eb, src_schop_sep | 1818-1851 | scholarly encyclopedia x2 + biographer] IEP credits an 'original insight' that the will is the thing in itself and Britannica traces influence on later existential philosophy, Freudian psychology, Nietzsche and Wagner; the support is retrospective reference-work praise plus the admiring biographer's account of influence, not a documented episode of creating. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 74/0.54 because the claim rests on retrospective praise and on a biographer from the Schopenhauer Society, and no source shows the process of invention.
      creative_originality: [70, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_schop_iep, src_schop_sep, src_schop_eb | 1813-1818 | scholarly encyclopedia x2 + biographer] IEP describes the 1813 dissertation as a systematic investigation of the principle of sufficient reason written as a response to Kant, and says the later system rests on it (SEP: the centrepiece of his later philosophy); he also attacked Fichte and Hegel for obscurity and published in a clear, direct prose style (IEP). Documented systematic argument; the clarity of his prose is not the same as rigour of proof, so the score is held at 70.
      analytical_rigor: [70, 0.55, "s", "A"],
      // [src_schop_iep, src_schop_eb, src_schop_sep | 1818-1844 | scholarly encyclopedia x2 + biographer] Britannica describes The World as Will and Representation as a four-part account running from knowledge through nature, aesthetics and ethics under one principle, and IEP says he combined Kant's idealism with the single insight that the will is the thing in itself; SEP notes later works re-presenting the system and its confirmation. Documented single-system construction across several domains; the merit of the system is not scored. RUBRIC_CORRECTION (lead review, before eligibility): systems_abstraction lowered from 76 / 0.6 to 72 / 0.54 because retrospective judgements of the system by encyclopedias rather than documented method.
      systems_abstraction: [72, 0.54, "s", "A"],
      // [src_schop_iep, src_schop_sep, src_schop_eb | 1809-1860 | scholarly encyclopedia x2 + biographer] A lifelong interest in Eastern thought begun through the Orientalist Friedrich Majer (IEP), reading of the Upanishads in his later years (SEP), study of medicine and natural sciences at Gottingen before philosophy (Britannica, SEP) and daily reading of The London Times at Frankfurt (SEP). Pattern of wide self-directed reading from several accounts; no single episode of inquiry is described in detail.
      curiosity: [70, 0.56, "s", "A"],
