    directoryVisible: true,
    sources: [{ id: "src_sancho_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Ignatius Sancho (Q1657368), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-05): description 'British composer, writer and grocer', born 1729, died 14 December 1780, no Korean label (the Korean name below is a recorded transliteration). Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1657368" }, { id: "src_sancho_carey", kind: "biography", title: "Brycchan Carey, 'Ignatius Sancho: African Composer and Man of Letters' (brycchancarey.com; a scholar's study page, read). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE (with the London Museum story below): said to be born into slavery on a ship crossing to the West Indies; as a child in Greenwich forced to work for three sisters; employed by the Montagu family as butler; ran a grocery at 19 Charles Street, Westminster; composed music, appeared on stage and entertained London's literary and artistic circles; painted by Gainsborough in Bath in 1768; his letters published in 1782 and reissued in five editions to 1803; to eighteenth-century opponents of the slave trade he symbolised the humanity of Africans. The page links to the scholarly debate on the reliability of Joseph Jekyll's memoir but does not describe it. Single-author scholar's website; short.", url: "https://www.brycchancarey.com/sancho" }, { id: "src_sancho_londonmuseum", kind: "institution", title: "London Museum, 'Ignatius Sancho: Writer and early Black voter' (London Stories page, read). Records that he taught himself to read after the sisters refused to teach him, that the Duke of Montagu lent him books, that he became butler to the Duchess Mary and later valet to the next Duke, married Ann Osbourne in 1758, opened a grocery near Parliament in 1774 with the Duke's help and voted as a householder; that he wrote letters and essays (some printed under the name Africanus) and plays, composed music, was friends with Garrick and Sterne and was painted by Gainsborough in 1768; that his 1766 letter urged Sterne to support abolition; that his Letters (1782) sold out and were reprinted; and that he was less hopeful than his publisher about English attitudes and still faced discrimination. Museum text: balanced, institutional.", url: "https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/collections/london-stories/ignatius-sancho-writer-first-black-voter/" }, { id: "src_sancho_rmg", kind: "institution", title: "Royal Museums Greenwich, collection record for the stipple-engraving frontispiece 'Ignatius Sancho, 1729-80' (read). Says the print is after an oil portrait by Thomas Gainsborough now in the National Gallery of Canada; that after Montagu's death in 1749 Sancho persuaded the widow to remove him from the sisters' household and she hired him as butler; that with Montagu family support he opened a Westminster grocery that sold slave-produced goods; that his property gave him the vote and the record calls him the second-earliest known Black person to vote in a British election, after John London in 1749; and that he was the first African in Britain to receive an obituary. Museum record; its 'second-earliest' voter claim differs from the 'first' claim in some other pages.", url: "https://www.rmg.co.uk/collections/objects/rmgc-object-254684" }, { id: "src_sancho_rutgers", kind: "institution", title: "Caroline Copeland, 'Ignatius Sancho and the Minuet', The Life and World of Ignatius Sancho (Rutgers-New Brunswick interdisciplinary research group; read). Says he published minuets for friends, family and patrons to dance, shows the title page 'Minuets, Cotillons & Country Dances... Composed by an African', dated [1767], printed for the author in London and dedicated to Henry, Duke of Buccleuch, with six minuets and eighteen cotillions and country dances (the page's own counts of minuets differ). It does not assess his musical training, reception or quality; the only evaluative comment concerns his letters.", url: "https://sancho.rutgers.edu/?p=826" }, { id: "src_sancho_letters", kind: "archive", title: "Letters of the Late Ignatius Sancho, an African, to which are prefixed Memoirs of his Life (third edition, London: J. Nichols, 1784; Project Gutenberg text of an Internet Archive scan; the Memoirs by Joseph Jekyll, the letter to Mr. Sterne and the letters of May-June 1780 on the state of the country and the Gordon Riots read, the rest searched only). SELF-REPORT and a prejudiced contemporary memoir: the Letters are his own words (the letter to Sterne is printed here with a date of 1776, whereas the London Museum dates it 1766 and Sterne died in 1768); Jekyll's Memoirs carry demeaning racial assumptions and anecdotes that scholars treat as unreliable, so only facts also given by another source are used and its anecdotes about his private life are excluded.", url: "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/66908" }, { id: "src_sancho_met", kind: "institution", title: "The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Open Access record for 'Ignatius Sancho' (object 376537, Francesco Bartolozzi, stipple engraving and etching, 1802, Harris Brisbane Dick Fund 1917; read via the Met Open Access API). Portrait source record for the image used.", url: "https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/376537" }],
    rows: {
      // [src_sancho_letters, src_sancho_londonmuseum, src_sancho_rmg | 1749-1774 | his own letter, museum text, museum record] After the Duke of Montagu died in 1749 he left the sisters' household and, per the Greenwich museum record, persuaded the widow to take him on as butler; in a letter to Laurence Sterne (1766 per the London Museum) he wrote unprompted to a famous author, urging him to give attention to slavery; in 1774 he and his wife set up the Westminster grocery. The letter to Sterne is his own text and the museum texts agree on the other moves, but the museum texts are short and the earlier events are documented mainly through the Montagu patronage, so confidence is moderate.
      proactive_agency: [64, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_sancho_carey, src_sancho_londonmuseum, src_sancho_rutgers | 1760s-1780 | scholar's page, museum text, university research page] Sources agree that alongside household service and then shopkeeping he composed and published dance music, wrote letters, essays and plays, appeared on stage and moved in London's literary and artistic circles. The breadth is documented by independent sources, but none describes the quality of each strand (the Rutgers page does not assess the music and the plays are only named), so the row stays moderate.
      cross_domain_range: [64, 0.48, "s", "A"],
      // [src_sancho_londonmuseum, src_sancho_letters | childhood-1770s | museum text, his own letter] The London Museum says that when the sisters refused to teach him he taught himself to read and that the Duke of Montagu lent him books; in his letter to Sterne he writes that he got a little reading and writing 'by unwearied application' and that his 'chief pleasure has been books'. Mostly his own description plus one museum summary. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): top-risk row; lowered from 66 / 0.50 to 62 / 0.45 because it leans on his own self-description and one unsourced museum statement, and Jekyll's memoir, the other source of this story, is unreliable.
      curiosity: [62, 0.45, "s", "A"],
      // [src_sancho_rmg, src_sancho_londonmuseum | 1749, 1766 | museum record, museum text] The Greenwich record says he persuaded the widowed Duchess to hire him, and the London Museum says Sterne was persuaded by his letter to take up the cause. Two single-sentence claims in museum texts, with no account of how he argued; the Sterne claim is not shown in the sources read.
      persuasiveness: [58, 0.4, "i", "A"],
    },
