      aesthetic_sensitivity: [70, 0.52, "s", "A"],
      // [src_osmanhamdi_shaw, src_osmanhamdi_eldem | 1880-1908 | scholarly abstract + journal preview] Shaw describes allegorical paintings that stage the Ottoman past with dignity and irony against Orientalist tropes, while Eldem's preview shows the Tortoise Trainer's composition traced to a French magazine engraving of 1869 and treats what it means as contested. He trained under French Orientalist painters and kept to their manner, so the originality lies in the use of that manner, not in a new style; both sources are partial (abstract and preview only).
      creative_originality: [58, 0.45, "s", "D"],
      // [src_osmanhamdi_mee, src_osmanhamdi_britannica, src_osmanhamdi_archaeology | 1881-1900 | journalism + encyclopedia + magazine review] He worked with the architect Alexander Vallaury on the museum building (Middle East Eye), co-wrote the Sidon report with Theodore Reinach (Britannica), and dealt with the University of Pennsylvania, whose Nippur leader arranged honorary degrees and bought his paintings to gain access (Archaeology Magazine). The last item is a critical reading of transactional co-operation, not a verdict on his character; the row records documented partnership with specialists and institutions.
