    sources: [{ id: "src_sakyapandita_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Sakya Pandita (Q982008), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-05): English label 'Sakya Pandita', description 'Tibetan Lama (1182-1251)', birth 1182 and death 1251 (year precision), images include the Met thangka. No Korean label. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q982008" }, { id: "src_sakyapandita_sep", kind: "biography", title: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, 'Sakya Pandita [sa skya pan di ta]' (Jonathan C. Gold; the whole entry read from the public page: life and works, the Treasury of Epistemological Reasoning, the Clear Differentiation of the Three Vows, Clarifying the Sage's Intent, the Gateway to Learning). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: the fourth of the five great throneholders of Sakya who 'established Sakya anew as a leader in the study of epistemology'; best known for five major works; the Treasury of Epistemological Reasoning is called 'one of Tibet's best known and most consequential philosophical achievements' but with 'an ambiguous legacy' and controversy from its debut; the Three Vows is 'a polemical treatise' and his 'most controversial work'; the Gateway to Learning is 'a rare example of a medieval treatise on the goals and means of education'; he studied with Sugataśri for three years and Śākyaśrī for five, retranslating Dharmakirti's Pramanavarttika with the latter; he was 'often criticized and sometimes lampooned for being stodgy, conservative, and self-satisfied'; the entry flags the hagiographical fancy in his early biographies and says it is not clear what terms followed his 1244 Mongol summons. Scholarly reference written by a modern academic; the assessments of his works are retrospective.", url: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sakya-pandita/" }, { id: "src_sakyapandita_encbuddhism", kind: "biography", title: "Encyclopedia of Buddhism (Macmillan), 'Sa skya Pandita (Sakya Pandita)' by Ronald M. Davidson, read in full via Encyclopedia.com. An independent scholarly entry: 'revered as the greatest early scholar of the Sa skya sect', fourth of the five great Sa skya teachers, 'noted for his conservative polemics against what he saw as unwarranted Tibetan innovations'; sent to Central Tibet in 1200 to study Yogacara, Madhyamaka and Dharmakirti's logic; his decisive influence was the Kashmiri master Śākyaśribhadra and his retinue, who taught him the Sanskrit curriculum of the great Indian monasteries including poetics; more than one hundred compositions; Jackson's five works of special influence, the Treasury of Aphoristic Jewels being 'best known' and 'still memorized by Tibetans'; the Treasury of Epistemology is 'dedicated to the refutation of the innovations of Tibetan scholars, especially' Chapa Chokyi Senge.", url: "https://www.encyclopedia.com/religion/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/sa-skya-paita-sakya-paita" }, { id: "src_sakyapandita_eightself", kind: "archive", title: "Lotsawa House, 'Eight Self-Assertions' by Sakya Pandita (verse translated by Adam Pearcey, 2025, from the Sakya Kabum; Tibetan text and English read in full). SELF-REPORT: a famous verse that uses the word 'I' eight times: 'I'm a grammarian. I'm a logician... In figurative expression, I'm without rival... Other so-called scholars are mere imitations.' The page's own note attributes it to him, while the Tibetan edition cited is the Sakya Kabum volume that carries the commentary on the verse and names 'Sa chen Kun dga' snying po and others', so the attribution rests on the tradition and the self-presentation is not independently checked.", url: "https://www.lotsawahouse.org/tibetan-masters/sakya-pandita/eight-self-assertions" }, { id: "src_sakyapandita_rigpawiki", kind: "biography", title: "Rangjung Yeshe Wiki (Dharma Dictionary), 'Sakya Pandita Kunga Gyeltsen', read in full: a traditional Sakya-lineage short biography (from the Sakya Tegchen Choling newsletter) with dream visions, miraculous birth and magical-test stories, plus brief factual notes (ordination by Shakya Shri Bhadra, Sakya throne at 25, Literary Works list). Hagiographic; used only to show how the tradition presents him and as a contrast to the scholarly sources. Nothing from its legendary passages is scored.", url: "https://rywiki.tsadra.org/index.php/Sapan" }, { id: "src_sakyapandita_metthangka", kind: "institution", title: "The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 'Sakya Pandita', thangka, Eastern Tibet, 18th century, distemper on cloth, accession 2006.285 (Open Access API record for object 73300 read live: isPublicDomain true; image 1168 x 1676 downloaded). Portrait holder record only; the museum's HTML object page is bot-walled, so only the API record was read.", url: "https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/73300" }],
